> Now we have a tough decision to make with just that tool, right at the beginning of the response process. Using only fast, local processing, we need to decide whether to: 
> 1. Query the LLM immediately. We should always ask for both an immediate response and a plan, which may involve one of more searches.
> 2. Perform a RAG, with one or more searches, before querying the LLM. Increased latency, but higher chance of success.
> 3. Do both simultaneously. Low latency, but high cost if we don't end up using that information.
> 
> This process should also learn. For example, if it uses word-based cues, perhaps the probability of these supporting each of the results should update in a Bayesian manner, and then we should use that information in combination with the general scoring logic to select a plan based on highest expected utility or lowest expected regret.
