**assistant**:
> ## Recommendation: start with a bounded **three-arm routing policy**
> 
> For each turn, locally choose one of:
> 
> 1. **Direct + research plan**: call the LLM immediately, requiring:
>    - a useful direct answer;
>    - a small structured `research_plan` (need-search?, queries, why, freshness/risk).
> 2. **Search-first RAG**: run 1–$k$ searches, then call the LLM with selected, provenance-labeled evidence.
> 3. **Parallel**: start direct LLM and search concurrently; use evidence only if it completes before answer synthesis / a bounded deadline.
> 
> Use explicit variants as separately learnable `PlanSpec`s, e.g. `direct-v1`, `web-rag-1-high-v1`, `parallel-web-rag-1-v1`.
> 
> ## Phase 0: make tool contracts actually interoperable
> 
> This must precede routing. The shown web-search module does not match `planner/types.py`:
> 
> - `Artifact` uses `id/kind/content/provenance/created_at`, but the tool uses `artifact_id/data/mime_type`.
> - `ToolResult` has no `status`, `data`, or `.failure()`.
> - `ToolConfig` has `options`, not `allowed_options`.
> - `ToolUseResponder` currently only injects `source_excerpts`, while web search emits artifacts.
> - `ToolUseResponder.chat_response()` references an undefined `record`.
> 
> Create one canonical adapter/converter at the tool boundary; do not support multiple artifact/result schemas inside responders.
> 
> ## Routing features: fast, local, inspectable
> 
> Build `ResearchNeedFeatures` from the latest user message plus context:
> 
> - freshness cues: “latest”, “current”, dates, prices, laws, people/office holders;
> - evidence cues: “sources”, “cite”, “verify”, “what does X say?”;
> - task class/risk level;
> - named entities, URLs, question form, token length;
> - prior conversation state and explicit user preference;
> - estimated search and model cost/latency.
> 
> These features should produce a **prior**, not a brittle rule. High-stakes or explicit-source requests can impose hard constraints: search-first, approved-domain search, or no-answer-without-evidence.
> 
> ## Learning policy
> 
> Maintain per-plan, contextual evidence:
> 
> \[
> P(\text{satisfied} \mid p,x),\quad
> P(\text{grounded} \mid p,x),\quad
> P(\text{failure} \mid p,x)
> \]
> 
> Start simply: Beta-Binomial counters keyed by `(plan_id, feature_bucket)`, with hierarchical shrinkage to plan-wide priors. Update separately from:
> 
> - thumbs: satisfaction only;
> - evaluator/source checks: grounding and quality;
> - execution records: failures, latency, and cost.
> 
> Select using expected utility:
> 
> \[
> EU(p)=w_sS+w_qQ+w_gG-w_fF-w_cC-w_lL+w_eU
> \]
> 
> For parallel plans, charge the expected cost of **both** calls. Use Thompson sampling or posterior sampling only when `risk_level` permits exploration.
> 
> ## Execution design
> 
> Add a `ResearchRouterResponder` / workflow template:
> 
> - **Direct:** direct model response includes structured research recommendation; persist it as an artifact.
> - **Search-first:** derive an initial deterministic search query from the user request; execute bounded search; extract minimal source excerpts/citations; synthesize with explicit evidence instructions.
> - **Parallel:** launch two cancellable operations; direct response is provisional internally, then synthesize if search returns before deadline. Never silently alter already-streamed visible text.
> 
> For v1, keep the final user-visible answer single-shot: **do not stream parallel plans until synthesis has committed**.
> 
> ## Delivery milestones
> 
> 1. Canonicalize tool contracts and add web-search integration tests with a fake OpenAI client.
> 2. Implement explicit direct/search/parallel plan templates and bounded search configuration.
> 3. Add router features and deterministic baseline policy.
> 4. Persist routing decisions, candidate scores, evidence, costs, and outcomes.
> 5. Add Bayesian contextual learning and offline replay evaluation before enabling exploration in production.
